{
 "id": "l43",
 "label": "43",
 "title": "Contractions",
 "summary": "Everyday shortened words: téka (wait!), téna (let's go!), ayóko (I don't like), pénge (give me), 'dî, 'nó, ikámo and more",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what a Tagalog contraction is and how to spot one",
  "Know the ten common contractions that can stand alone as a reply or command",
  "Use 'dî and 'nó as tag questions (right?)",
  "Recognise ikámo / ikáko (you said / I said) and when they are used",
  "Know three more ways to say give me: pénge, éka and kaná"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Saying more with less",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When we talk or write to someone we know very well, we often **skip words or syllables** or **press them together** instead of saying them in full. Contractions let us say or write more in less time. A contraction is made by shortening a word, a syllable or a group of words, or by leaving out some letters, to make a **new, shorter word**."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "English has contractions too",
     "t": "*Don't*, *let's*, *isn't*, *we're* and *he's* are English contractions, and they follow certain rules. In Tagalog, the letters and syllables that are left out or changed can **vary from speaker to speaker**."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Here is how the book takes apart **téka** (wait!), which comes from **hintáy ka**:"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "hintáy ka → téka",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Full",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "hin",
         "r": "other"
        },
        {
         "t": "táy",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "ka",
         "r": "pron"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Step 1",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "(hin dropped)",
         "r": "other"
        },
        {
         "t": "tay",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "ka",
         "r": "pron"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Step 2",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "tay → te",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "+ ka",
         "r": "pron"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Result",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "téka",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "The syllable hin is left out, and tay changes to te and joins ka."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "How to spot a contraction",
     "t": "A simple rule: Tagalog contractions are **not regular words** with affixes or root words you can recognise. If a short everyday word has no root you know and no prefix or suffix, it may be a contraction."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Language keeps changing",
     "t": "Once you have learned the common contractions below, look out for **new ones used by the younger generation**. Some of them may creep into the language and become accepted."
    }
   ]
  },
